<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Picentes</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 268 BCE (approx.) · capital Asculum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Italic people of the central Adriatic coast, said to have followed a woodpecker to their new land, whose warrior culture lasted until Rome conquered them in 268 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Picentes lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picenum">Picenum</a>, the coastal strip and hills of the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a>, from the early 1st millennium BCE. Roman tradition said they descended from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sabines">Sabines</a> who left home in a 'sacred spring' migration, guided by a woodpecker (picus), the bird of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mars-mythology">Mars</a>. Archaeologists call their culture the Picene culture.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Picene society was dominated by warrior elites buried with helmets, weapons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> and amber from the Baltic. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-picene-language">South Picene</a> inscriptions, in a Sabellic language written in a distinctive alphabet, appear on stone stelae, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novilara-stele">Novilara stele</a> in the north bears a still undeciphered text. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascoli-piceno">Asculum</a> was their main centre, and they traded across the Adriatic and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Picentes allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 299 BCE but revolted in 269 BCE and were conquered in 268 BCE; part of the population was moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-salerno">Gulf of Salerno</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascoli-piceno">Asculum</a> became an allied city, and its massacre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 91 BCE began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picenum">Picenum</a> became part of Augustus's Regio V.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/picentes.jpg" alt="Picentes" width="320" height="389"><figcaption>Image: Dbachmann; derivative work by Ewan ar born translator: Manlleus (ca) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iron_Age_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 268 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Asculum</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Picene polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>South Picene</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Picenes, Piceni</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-534&amp;focus=picentes" aria-label="Picentes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4964 592 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Picentes lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5336 935h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5284 848h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5284 848h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Italy. <a href="/atlas?year=-534&amp;focus=picentes">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

